Now the main integration test with MockMvc. This will be long; split into a support base class and two test classes. Let me create a support class `KilnApiSupport` with helper methods (createProgram, createBatch, upload, release, etc.) as a @SpringBootTest? No — helpers need MockMvc which is per-class. I'll make an abstract class annotated with @SpringBootTest/@AutoConfigureMockMvc that subclasses extend; Spring runs tests in subclasses with the same context config (same DB name). But then @AfterAll SHUTDOWN per subclass... context shared across subclasses with same configuration — SHUTDOWN in one breaks the other. Give each concrete class its own DB name via properties on the subclass. Abstract class without @SpringBootTest properties; subclasses add @SpringBootTest(properties=...)? Annotations are inherited in Spring test (@SpringBootTest is @Inherited? Yes, @SpringBootTest is inherited). Properties attribute inherited too unless overridden — subclass can redeclare @SpringBootTest with different properties. Simpler: put helpers as instance methods in abstract class, and each concrete class annotated separately with its own DB name. The abstract class carries @SpringBootTest + @AutoConfigureMockMvc; subclass adds @SpringBootTest(properties=...) to override. Hmm, redeclaring on subclass replaces inherited annotation. That works.

Actually simpler: just two fully separate test classes with duplicated small helpers? Duplication is ugly. Let me do the abstract base approach:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
abstract class KilnApiIT {
    @Autowired MockMvc mvc;
    @Autowired JdbcTemplate jdbc;
    @Autowired ObjectMapper om;
    @Autowired MutableClock clock;

    @TestConfiguration
    static class ClockConfig { @Bean @Primary MutableClock mutableClock() {...} }

    @BeforeEach void cleanup() { jdbc.update("DELETE FROM ...") x7 }

    helpers: createProgram(), createBatch(no, programId), upload(batchId, reqId, readingsJson)->ResultActions, readingsJson(offsets/temps), release(batchId), etc.
}
```

Wait — nested @TestConfiguration in abstract class: is it picked up for subclasses? Nested static @TestConfiguration classes are detected relative to the test class... Spring Boot finds nested @TestConfiguration in the test class hierarchy? The docs: "@TestConfiguration ... can be used ... as nested classes within a test class". Detection uses the actual test class and its superclasses? I believe nested test configuration is searched on the test class and its superclasses (TestContextBootstrapper looks at getEnclosingClass hierarchy... Actually it searches the test class's nested classes and those of superclasses? Let me not risk it: use @Import(TestClockConfig.class) on the abstract class with a top-level TestClockConfig in support package. @Import is inherited via @SpringBootTest context merging? Annotations on superclasses are honored for @Import (it's @Inherited? @Import isn't @Inherited but Spring's test context merges annotations from superclasses via MergedAnnotations with search hierarchy). Yes, spring-test uses MergedAnnotations.from(type, SearchStrategy.TYPE_HIERARCHY) for most annotations. Safe.

So create support/TestClockConfig.java:

```java
@TestConfiguration
public class TestClockConfig {
    @Bean
    @Primary
    public MutableClock mutableClock() {
        return new MutableClock(Instant.parse("2026-02-01T00:00:00Z"));
    }
}
```

Abstract base KilnApiIT with @SpringBootTest @AutoConfigureMockMvc @Import(TestClockConfig.class). Subclasses: KilnFlowIT with properties DB kiln_test_flow; AnomalyRefireIT with kiln_test_anomaly; ReleaseConcurrencyIT separate (service-level, no MockMvc needed but fine to reuse base) with kiln_test_conc.

@AfterAll SHUTDOWN: instance @AfterAll must be static; jdbc is instance. Use @AfterAll static with... simpler: implement per-class `@AfterAll static void shutdown(@Autowired?)` — not supported. Alternative: register shutdown via context closed event? Simplest reliable: in each concrete class, `@AfterAll` static can't access jdbc. Use `@PreDestroy`? Or make cleanup via SmartLifecycle... 

Alternative: after all tests in a class, execute SHUTDOWN using a static JdbcTemplate captured in @BeforeAll/@PostConstruct into a static field. E.g. in base class:

```java
private static JdbcTemplate staticJdbc;
@Autowired void initJdbc(JdbcTemplate jdbc) { KilnApiIT.staticJdbc = jdbc; }
@AfterAll static void shutdownDb() { if (staticJdbc != null) staticJdbc.execute("SHUTDOWN"); }
```

But base class @AfterAll runs for each concrete subclass; each subclass has its own context (different DB name property) → its own SHUTDOWN. static field shared across subclasses — but contexts are created per subclass before its tests run, so staticJdbc points to the current context's template at that time. Sequential execution (no parallel test classes by default) makes this safe. OK.

Hmm wait — do subclasses with different properties get different contexts? Yes, properties differ → different context cache keys → separate contexts, each with own H2 mem DB (different names anyway).

Concurrency test: doesn't need MockMvc but inheriting is fine.

