@Autowired ReleaseService releaseService;
@Autowired ReadingService readingService;
@Autowired ReleaseRepository releaseRepository;
@Autowired ReadingRepository readingRepository;

@Test
void concurrentReleasesProduceSingleRecord() throws Exception {
    long programId = createProgram("GLAZE-J", 1);
    long batchId = createBatch("B-CONC-1", programId);
    upload(batchId, "req-c1", fullTrajectory()).andExpect(status().isOk());

    int threads = 4;
    ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(threads);
    CountDownLatch ready = new CountDownLatch(threads);
    CountDownLatch start = new CountDownLatch(1);
    List<Future<String>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < threads; i++) {
        futures.add(pool.submit(() -> {
            ready.countDown();
            start.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
            try {
                releaseService.release(batchId, "qc-wang");
                return "OK";
            } catch (ApiException e) {
                return String.valueOf(e.status().value());
            }
        }));
    }
    assertTrue(ready.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS));
    start.countDown();
    List<String> results = new ArrayList<>();
    for (Future<String> f : futures) results.add(f.get(15, TimeUnit.SECONDS));
    pool.shutdownNow();

    assertEquals(1, results.stream().filter("OK"::equals).count());
    assertEquals(threads - 1, results.stream().filter("409"::equals).count());
    assertEquals(1, releaseRepository.countByBatch(batchId));
}
```

Hmm — 4 concurrent releases: first acquires lock, validates, inserts, commits. Others wait on FOR UPDATE lock. H2 default lock timeout: I need to check — H2's default LOCK_TIMEOUT is 2000 ms? If waiting thread times out, it throws JdbcSQLTimeoutException → not ApiException → future.get throws ExecutionException. To be safe, catch Exception and return class name; assert outcomes are OK or 409 only. If lock timeout occurs, test fails — transactions are quick (<100ms), so waits are short. But 4 threads × validation each... only the lock holder does work; others just wait. Should be fine.

Actually wait: there's a subtlety — threads that get 409: after first commits, second acquires lock, reads status RELEASED → ApiException 409. Good.

